### Books — /books
#
# "Create a book" stores the new book's id, which this file and loans.http then use. Nothing here
# needs a token: the API has no authentication of its own. X-Actor is what the audit trail records,
# and it is self-asserted — the trail stores a claim, not a verified identity.
#
# Deactivating a book is in withdrawal.http rather than at the end of this file: it is refused while
# copies are out, so running it here would withdraw the title before loans.http borrowed from it.

### Create a book — 201, or 409 on a re-run.
# totalCopies is 1 on purpose: it is what makes a second loan attempt demonstrate the availability
# refusal rather than quietly succeeding. The ISBN is validated by checksum and stored normalised,
# so 978-0-13-475759-9 and 9780134757599 are the same book and the second is a 409.
#
# That also means running this twice answers 409 — correctly. Change isbn in http-client.env.json
# to seed another title. (Bruno's collection mints a fresh one per run with a pre-request script;
# .http files have no equivalent hook, so here it is a variable you edit.)
POST {{host}}/books
X-Actor: {{actor}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Refactoring",
  "isbn": "{{isbn}}",
  "author": "Martin Fowler",
  "totalCopies": 1
}

> {%
    if (response.body.data && response.body.data.id) {
        client.global.set("bookId", response.body.data.id);
        client.log("Stored bookId " + response.body.data.id);
    }
%}

### List the catalogue — 200. Served through Redis when the page is cached.
# pageNumber starts at 1 and pageSize is capped at 200; both are validated, so 0 or 100000 answers
# 400 rather than being silently clamped. Deactivated books are excluded unless asked for.
GET {{host}}/books?pageNumber=1&pageSize=20&title=Refactoring&includeInactive=false
X-Actor: {{actor}}

### Read one book — 200, or 404.
# Deactivated books are included here, unlike in the listing: a caller asking by identifier is not
# browsing, and hiding a withdrawn title would only make it look deleted.
GET {{host}}/books/{{bookId}}
X-Actor: {{actor}}

### Update a book — 200.
# PATCH semantics: isbn and author are not sent, so they are left alone rather than blanked. A
# request that sends nothing at all is a 400.
#
# totalCopies moves the available count by the same delta, atomically, so a copy out on loan stays
# out. Lowering it below the copies currently lent is a 422 — well-formed, colliding with nothing,
# refused by a rule about the value itself.
PATCH {{host}}/books/{{bookId}}
X-Actor: {{actor}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Refactoring, second edition",
  "totalCopies": 3
}

### Read availability — 200. Cached, and advisory.
# calculatedAt says when the figure was taken; canBeLoaned describes a moment that has already
# passed. Nothing in the loan path consults this — whether a copy can be taken is decided inside
# POST /loans by an atomic conditional UPDATE. Run this before and after lending to see it refresh.
GET {{host}}/books/{{bookId}}/availability
X-Actor: {{actor}}

### Read the loan history — 200.
# Every loan ever made of this book, in every state, newest first. There is no way to exclude the
# terminal states wholesale, only to narrow to one with status=Active|Returned|Cancelled.
GET {{host}}/books/{{bookId}}/history?pageNumber=1&pageSize=20
X-Actor: {{actor}}
